{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>And Jacob dwelt, etc.</b> After He wrote for you the settlements of Esau and his generations in brief form — for they were not important enough to set out in detail how they settled and the order of their wars, how they dispossessed the Horite — He set out for you the settlements of Jacob and his generations at length, the full unfolding of their cause, because they are important before the Omnipresent to elaborate upon. And so you find in the ten generations from Adam to Noah: so-and-so begot so-and-so, but when he reaches Noah he elaborates. And likewise in the ten generations from Noah to Abraham he is brief, but once he reaches Abraham he elaborates. A parable: a pearl that fell into the sand — a man sifts the sand and winnows it with a sieve until he finds the pearl, and once he finds it he throws away the handfuls from his hand and takes the pearl. Another interpretation — <b>And Jacob dwelt:</b> this flax-driver — his camels entered loaded with flax. The potter said: Where will all this flax go in? One clever fellow answered him: One spark comes out of your bellows and burns up the lot. So Jacob saw all the chieftains written above and wondered, saying: Who can conquer all of them? What is written below? ״אֵלֶּה תּוֹלְדוֹת יַעֲקֹב יוֹסֵף״ — as it is written ״וְהָיָה בֵית יַעֲקֹב אֵשׁ וּבֵית יוֹסֵף לֶהָבָה וּבֵית עֵשָׂו לְקַשׁ״ (Obadiah 1) — a spark goes out from Joseph that consumes and burns up all of them."], :book :genesis, :voice :rashi, :lang :en, :chapter 37, :notes {}, :source-witnesses nil, :timestamp "2026-08-02T09:02:24.644454971Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}